30.3  Merge or rebase?

TL;DR
  - Both bring another branch's work into yours. Merge adds a commit with two
    parents and keeps history as it happened. Rebase replays your commits on top
    of the other branch: a straight line.
  - Rebase gives your commits new hashes. Rebase only commits nobody else has:
    your local or personal branches.
  - A conflict means both sides changed the same lines: edit the file, git add
    it, then continue.

      git merge main                 # or: git rebase main
      git status                     # files in conflict
      git merge --continue           # or: git rebase --continue / --abort

On your machine
  Files     /etc/gitconfig, ~/.gitconfig
  Identity  name and email set
  Settings  init.defaultbranch = main
            pull.rebase = true
            core.autocrlf = input
            credential.helper = store (passwords in plain text in
            ~/.git-credentials)
  Aliases   st, lg
  Includes  1 more file, read under conditions (include, includeIf)

Pitfalls
  - After rebasing a branch you already pushed, a plain push is rejected;
    git push --force-with-lease replaces it, and breaks others' copies.
  - git rebase -i main lets you squash, reorder or reword commits before sharing
    them; don't do it on shared history.
  - Conflict markers left in a file (<<<<<<<) still commit: search for them
    before git add.
  - merge.conflictstyle = zdiff3 shows the common ancestor in conflicts: much
    easier to resolve.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with atfq 30.3 --why

